      "title": "Read me first",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# The reel\n\nFive nodes, left to right, and each one is a sentence. Press **Run** once; they\ngo in order on their own.\n\n| | | |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Project** | this is my material |\n| 2 | **Align** | my words become timings |\n| 3 | **Shot Table** | I decide what is seen |\n| 4 | **Subtitles** | the words become captions |\n| 5 | **Render Reel** | the reel is made |\n\n## The edit is not in this graph\n\nOpen the **Storyline** tab in the sidebar (the left-hand rail). That is where\nscenes get their pictures: click a scene, click a picture. It writes\n`shots.csv`, which is the file this graph reads.\n\nThere is nothing to edit inside the Shot Table node. It compiles the table and\nprints the report; the report is worth reading, and it is where the tool tells\nyou an image is too small before you have spent a render finding out.\n\n## Where the recording comes from\n\nNot from here. Open `voice.json` first, shape your take, and let **MemoActs -\nSet Narration** write it into a project. Every timing below is measured against\nwhat that graph produced, so the pace is decided there and nowhere later.\n\n## What costs what\n\n**Align** is the only slow step -- about 90 seconds the first time, then\nnothing: it is cached on the script and the recording, so changing a picture\nand running again does not pay for it twice.\n\n**Render Reel** is the long one, and roughly 61 ms a frame. A 22-second reel is\nabout 40 seconds of rendering. An `effect_preset` other than `none` multiplies\nthat -- the tooltip carries the measured figure for each look.\n\n**Preview Shot** renders one scene, without audio or captions. Use it to check\na framing rather than re-rendering the reel.\n\n## If it stops\n\n- *no narration.\\** -- the project has no recording yet. That is `voice.json`.\n- *script.md has no shots* -- the scene headings are missing or escaped. A\n  heading is `## S01` at the start of a line; `\\## S01` is not one.\n- *source supplies only N px* -- the picture is smaller than the frame. It\n  still renders; `on_upscale` decides whether that warns, refuses, or passes.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
